@flexiarg cycle-machine/runtime-restoration
@title Runtime Restoration: Reload the Shared JVM from Its Own Checkout, Whole
@keywords shared-jvm, reload, load-file, worktree, classpath, restore, master, stale-namespace
@audience cycle-machine builders, APM maintainers, operators
@tone formal-analytic
@grade technique
@why cycle-machine/step-machine
@see-also coordination/session-durability-check
@theory-grounding [I1(Boundary) A4(Degradation-detectable)]

! conclusion:
  If you want a durable step machine to survive the one disruption it cannot detect from its state file — a stale or foreign namespace in the process that runs it — then provide one restoration command that reloads every cycle namespace from the canonical checkout, verifies each against the checkout's git HEAD, and re-adopts the registered coordinators, and make that command the only sanctioned way to recover the runtime.

  + context: The step machine's pre-state digest detects a state that moved. It cannot detect code that moved: a namespace loaded from another worktree, or a namespace that was never reloaded after master advanced. Both present as "the system has a bug" and were diagnosed as such, twice in one morning.

  + IF:
    a cycle is driven by a long-lived shared JVM that is hot-loaded from several places by several agents

  + HOWEVER:
    a partial restore (one namespace) leaves the others stale; a restore that does not verify leaves no way to tell a restored runtime from a broken one; and a restore that does not re-adopt the coordinators leaves registered-but-unsupervised machines (enabled? true, nobody ticking)

  + THEN:
    implement scripts/restore-apm-runtime.sh (or extend restore-http-routes.sh) to:
      1. refuse to run unless the control checkout is on master and clean for the namespaces it will load;
      2. (require 'ns :reload) every namespace under futon3c.apm.* plus futon3c.transport.http, in dependency order, from the classpath — never load-file;
      3. for each reloaded namespace, compare a digest of the loaded source (the JVM can report the file it loaded) to the checkout's blob; report any mismatch as a finding;
      4. probe one master-only route and one coordinator status;
      5. re-adopt every registry entry with :coordinator/enabled? true into the supervisor, and report which were orphaned before;
      6. print a one-line receipt {:restored [...] :mismatched [...] :readopted [...]} and persist it under data/apm-runtime/restorations/.
    Any other recovery path (load-file of a worktree, starting a second JVM, leaving a fork alive) is a violation per the 2026-08-23 policy, not an alternative.

    + VALIDATION:
      - [ ] A script exists that reloads ALL futon3c.apm.* namespaces, not just transport.http.
      - [ ] It refuses when the checkout is not master or has uncommitted changes to the namespaces it loads.
      - [ ] It uses require :reload through the classpath; grep shows no load-file.
      - [ ] It re-adopts enabled registry entries and reports orphaned ones.
      - [ ] It emits and persists a receipt naming restored / mismatched / readopted.
      - [ ] Running it twice in a row produces an identical second receipt (idempotent).

  + BECAUSE:
    code staleness is invisible to the state machine by construction, so it needs its own detector; one command with a receipt turns "is the JVM healthy?" from a question only Joe can answer by restarting it into a check any agent can run and any reviewer can audit

    + evidence: 2026-08-22/23: load-file of a 56-commits-behind worktree replaced transport.http in the :7070 JVM; every master-only route answered Unknown endpoint twice in one morning. 16058df7 "Recover library coordinator in the shared JVM": a stale futon3c.apm.* namespace was still live after restore-http-routes.sh, which reloads only transport.http

    + RESULT:
      the step-machine's disruption list gains a fourth entry (namespace reload) with a sanctioned remedy; cycle-machine/disruption-soak can inject it
